@@ -0,0 +1,43 @@
+---
+id: dl-001-defer-tsc-typechecking-of-test-files-running-natively-via-node-test
+type: decision-log
+title: "Defer tsc typechecking of test files running natively via node --test"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`package.json`'s only script is `"test": "node --test"`: there is no build step before tests
+run. Node 22's native TypeScript support lets `node --test` execute `.test.ts` files directly,
+but only if their relative imports use the real `.ts` extension on disk (e.g.
+`from './index.ts'`) — the `.js`-extension convention `tsconfig.json`'s `NodeNext` module
+resolution expects assumes a prior `tsc` build that doesn't happen here.
+
+Running `tsc --noEmit` against the project (to implement `resolvePointer` in
+`src/index.ts`) therefore fails on `src/index.test.ts`: `node:test`/`node:assert` are unresolved
+without `@types/node`, and the `./index.ts` import is rejected with TS5097 unless
+`allowImportingTsExtensions` is enabled — which TypeScript only allows when the project sets
+`noEmit` or `emitDeclarationOnly`. This project's `tsconfig.json` instead sets `declaration: true`
+and `outDir: dist`, because `json-toolkit` is published as compiled JS for other projects to
+import (per `README.md`), so flipping to `noEmit` would be wrong for the real build.
+
+## Decision
+
+Leave `tsconfig.json` and `package.json` as they are for now: no `typescript`/`@types/node`
+devDependency, no `typecheck` script, no second tsconfig for the build. `src/index.ts` itself
+(the only file that is actually published) was verified to compile with zero errors under the
+existing strict `tsconfig.json`, using a temporary, uncommitted `typescript` install. Correctness
+of the new behaviour is instead verified by the `node --test` suite, which runs the TypeScript
+source directly and passes.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- `tsc` cannot currently typecheck `src/*.test.ts` files; a type error introduced only in a test
+  file would not be caught until it broke something at runtime.
+- The next task that touches the build setup should decide deliberately between: (a) a dedicated
+  `tsconfig.build.json` for `dist` emission plus a root `tsconfig.json` with `noEmit: true` and
+  `allowImportingTsExtensions: true` for dev typechecking of both `src` and tests, or (b) adding a
+  real compile step before `node --test` runs, importing compiled `.js` output instead of `.ts`
+  source. Either requires adding `@types/node` as a devDependency.
+- Until then, test files should keep importing sibling modules with the real `.ts` extension, to
+  match how `node --test` actually resolves them.
